   512	
   513	### 4.6 Ancestor staging obligations
   514	
   515	Two inputs reach Cargo with **no byte inside `R` naming them**, so no walk of
   516	the snapshot can see them. Both are manager staging obligations, checked from
   517	the parent of `R` upward to the filesystem root before the graph phase, and both
   518	fail `build_execution_control_unavailable` when they cannot be met:
   519	
   520	1. no ancestor directory of `R` contains a `.cargo` directory;
   521	2. no ancestor directory of `R` contains a `Cargo.toml`.
   522	
   523	Obligation 1 is unchanged. **Measured**: a `.cargo/config.toml` in an ancestor
   524	directory above the build root is discovered and applied.
   525	
   526	Obligation 2 is new and is the answer to a measured escape. **Measured**: with
   527	`parent/Cargo.toml` carrying `[workspace] members = ["build_root"]` and
   528	`[patch.crates-io] cfg-if = { path = "evil" }`, where `parent/evil` is an
   529	outside path package declaring `build = "build.rs"`, the graph phase reported
   530	`workspace_root` = `parent`, `workspace_members` of length **1** whose single
   531	element **is** the `R` package, `resolve.root` equal to that same package, and a
   532	`cfg-if` package whose `manifest_path` is `parent/evil/Cargo.toml` with target
   533	kinds `[["lib"], ["custom-build"]]`. G1 as written passes that shape. The
   534	redirect is caught only downstream, after Cargo has read the outside tree.
   535	Removing the ancestor manifest moved `workspace_root` back to `R` in the same
   536	fixture.
   537	
   538	### 4.7 What Stage P proves, and what it does not
   539	
   540	It proves that every filesystem path Cargo can reach from snapshot bytes lies
   541	inside `R`, decided before Cargo starts, on declared strings rather than on
   542	resolved output. Together with section 4.6 it closes the two origins that are
   543	not declared in snapshot bytes at all.
   544	
   545	It does not replace the graph phase. Sections 7.2 and 7.4 still run, and after
   546	Stage P their role is a **consistency cross-check** rather than the primary
   547	gate: every `manifest_path` and every target `src_path` the graph reports MUST
   548	already be a member of the Stage P admitted set, and `workspace_root` MUST equal
   549	`R`. A disagreement means Cargo reached a file the manager did not admit; it is
   550	`build_rust_graph_inconsistent`, it is a defect in the manager rather than a
   551	property of the package, and it MUST NOT be reported as a package rejection.
   552	
   553	It is not a containment guarantee. It bounds what Cargo *resolves*; it does not
   554	bound what `rustc` reads through `include_str!`, which stays the stated residual
   555	exposure of section 12.
   815	   whole lock. **Measured**: a root with `itoa` optional behind `extra` locks
   816	   `cfg-if`, `itoa`, `optreg`; plain `cargo vendor` vendors `cfg-if` and `itoa`;
   817	   the `--all-features` graph reports all three and the default-feature build
   818	   exits 0. `cargo vendor` has no `--all-features` flag — **measured**,
   819	   `error: unexpected argument '--all-features' found`.
   820	
   821	The matrix is therefore host-independent and deliberately over-approximating: a
   822	dependency that would only be built on another platform, or only under a feature
   823	this build does not enable, still rejects the command. Two further costs are
   824	stated rather than hidden: `--all-features` can make resolution fail on a
   825	package whose features are mutually exclusive, and it can pull a build-script
   826	dependency into the graph that the default build would never touch. Both are
